From 4101ef74427b9e980f463ec8f999b23dd3f42188 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Bloomin <452472+daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 12:41:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(retirement): a running background review holds the
 backend retirement fence [daveey]

Background reviews (the post-turn memory/skill review fork) ran on a daemon
thread with no session turn lease and no admission reservation, so the idle
proof read a backend mid-review as idle. On 2026-10-28 04:38 PDT the skew-exit
retirement of an SSH-isolated backend committed while two reviews were
mid-call; the shutdown cancelled both (reason=session_ended phase=model_wait)
and a skill change was lost.

- RetirementFence.begin/end_background_review: each spawned review holds the
  fence for its whole run (refused once a permit is issued, so a retiring
  backend skips the review cleanly instead of cutting it off at shutdown).
- prepare() refuses while a review runs. Past BACKGROUND_REVIEW_WAIT_S (300 s)
  it sets the hold's wind_down, and the fork stops before its next provider
  call (begin_iteration, review_retirement_wind_down), never mid-call. Past
  BACKGROUND_REVIEW_HARD_CAP_S (900 s) a wedged review stops counting, so it
  cannot starve retirement.
- busy_ledger reports background_review:<n> (idle-exit watchdog, /api/health/idle).

The hermes-sync drain-window probe is deliberately unchanged: the tree move
did not kill the reviews, the later backend retirement did, and gating the
drain on reviews would bring back update starvation.
